WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.199 --> 00:00:06.200
So now we have an understanding of our pawn types, controllers, animation, saving and loading,

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.199
the game world setup and other gameplay types that allow us to achieve our desired goals.

3
00:00:10.199 --> 00:00:16.199
With all that in mind, we should maybe take a look to see if whether or not our technical designs need us to override any engine specific classes,

4
00:00:16.199 --> 00:00:20.199
specifically the complex classes such as the game instance.

5
00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:24.649
This is where the programming subsystems really do come in handy.

6
00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:28.649
Programming subsystems allow us as developers to make use of instantiated classes

7
00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:33.450
to implement functionality without needing to override engine classes.

8
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.450
The lifetime of a subsystem is managed. This means that we have a definitive start and end

9
00:00:37.450 --> 00:00:40.450
that we can use to drive any functionality that we want.

10
00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:47.340
The lifespan of a subsystem will vary depending on which subsystem you use, but we'll cover those in just a second.

11
00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:51.409
So to put it into better context, let's say that we're considering a piece of functionality

12
00:00:51.409 --> 00:00:54.409
and we've identified that the game instance fits our needs.

13
00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:59.409
This is a really complex class and whilst it is perfectly fine to add whatever functionality we want,

14
00:00:59.409 --> 00:01:03.409
it might bloat our class beyond what we really need it to, and if we haven't already overridden it,

15
00:01:03.409 --> 00:01:07.409
then it might feel a bit overkill to override it to add our new functionality.

16
00:01:07.409 --> 00:01:11.019
This is where the benefits of a programming subsystem come into play.

17
00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:15.019
We can simply extend from a chosen subsystem class and piggyback on the back of that

18
00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:18.019
for its initialisation to implement our own functionality.

19
00:01:18.019 --> 00:01:22.819
Furthermore, programming subsystems provide easy to use extension points

20
00:01:22.819 --> 00:01:26.819
where developers can get Blueprint and Python exposure straight away.

21
00:01:26.819 --> 00:01:30.819
And it's all done without having to override any engine classes in the process.

22
00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:34.819
So as we can see, the Unreal Engine offers us some supported subsystems straight out of the box.

23
00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:38.819
And as can be seen here, all we need to do is identify our desired subsystem

24
00:01:38.819 --> 00:01:40.819
and simply inherit from that class.

25
00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:45.840
As we've discussed, programming subsystems have got a managed lifetime.

26
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.840
However, their lifetime will vary depending on which subsystem you use.

27
00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.480
So if we take the Engine and Editor subsystems as an example,

28
00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:56.480
these share common initialisation and de-initialisation points.

29
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.510
Both of their subsystems will be spanned up and spanned down

30
00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.510
when their corresponding modules are created and turned down.

31
00:02:02.510 --> 00:02:06.700
To give access to these in C++, we simply use the global GEngine

32
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.699
or GEditor pointers that are available to programmers.

33
00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:15.759
As for the game instance subsystem, this is spun up and spun down alongside the game instance itself,

34
00:02:15.759 --> 00:02:17.759
which we've already covered previously.

35
00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:21.020
To grab a hold of the game instance subsystem, all we simply need to do

36
00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:24.020
is grab a reference to the game instance from our world.

37
00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.979
The local player is possibly a bit more unique

38
00:02:27.979 --> 00:02:30.979
in that the local player is created when the player enters the world

39
00:02:30.979 --> 00:02:32.979
and is held by the player's player controller.

40
00:02:32.979 --> 00:02:36.979
It's worth noting that the subsystem will only exist on the server or owning client,

41
00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:41.300
as the player controllers are not replicated to any remote clients.

42
00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:45.300
Lastly, we can go over how to access a subsystem from outside of C++.

43
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:50.810
As we've already covered, programming subsystems can be accessed via Blueprint or Python.

44
00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:54.479
Within Blueprint, all we simply have to do is grab our desired subsystem

45
00:02:54.479 --> 00:02:59.150
and call whatever function that we have exposed for Blueprint to use.

46
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:02.150
As for Python, you can follow the example at the bottom of the slide.

47
00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:04.439
It's important to note that the Python integration

48
00:03:04.439 --> 00:03:08.439
is currently considered an experimental feature in the engine.

49
00:03:08.439 --> 00:03:12.300
And that concludes introduction to gameplay in Unreal Engine.

50
00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:16.620
Today, we learned about the core classes involved in Unreal's gameplay framework,

51
00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:22.099
such as the player and player controller, as well as how to set up a game world.

52
00:03:22.099 --> 00:03:27.099
We've looked at the basic building blocks of animation, such as montages and skeletons.

53
00:03:27.099 --> 00:03:29.099
And we've learned about how Unreal handles input

54
00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:33.099
and what systems Unreal provides when going about building our control schemes.

55
00:03:33.099 --> 00:03:35.460
We've covered delegates and events,

56
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:39.460
as well as helper libraries that come with Unreal straight out of the box.

57
00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.840
We looked at serialization before wrapping up with programming subsystems.

58
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:46.840
Thanks so much for watching, and have a fantastic day.
